# custom-lib — app/src/lib/text.ts has a fixed API

## Context

`app/src/lib/text.ts` is an in-house utility file, **not** lodash/underscore.
Its entire supported surface is exactly three functions. Assuming a
lodash-style helper exists here is a common hallucination this rule exists to
prevent.

## Rule

- The **only** functions that exist in `app/src/lib/text.ts` are:
  - `slugify(input: string): string`
  - `truncate(input: string, maxLength: number, suffix?: string): string`
  - `normalizeSpaces(input: string): string`
- Do **not** assume or import helpers like `capitalize`, `camelCase`,
  `deburr`, `kebabCase`, `startCase`, etc. — they do not exist in this repo.
- If a task genuinely needs a new text helper, **add it explicitly** to
  `app/src/lib/text.ts` with a named export and a colocated test in
  `app/src/lib/text.test.ts` — don't assume it or silently import from a
  library.
- Do not change the existing three functions' signatures; only add to the
  file.

## How to verify

1. `grep -rnE "import .*(capitalize|camelCase|deburr|kebabCase|startCase)" app/src`
   → no matches, and
   `grep -rnE "(capitalize|camelCase|deburr|kebabCase|startCase)\s*\(" app/src`
   → no matches. (These names DO legitimately appear in a warning comment
   inside `text.ts` itself — what must never exist are imports or call sites
   of them, so grep for those specifically. Exception: a matching new export
   just added deliberately, with a test.)
2. Every import from `./lib/text.js` / `./text.js` only names
   `slugify`, `truncate`, or `normalizeSpaces` (or a newly-added, tested
   export).
3. `cd app && npm test` covers any newly added helper.
